{"id":"e01t3","name":"Driver Payout Drops","lang":"sql","entry":null,"params":[],"limit_s":10,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>Drivers are paid every week. A sudden drop of the weekly payout often means that a driver is about to leave the platform, so the retention team wants a list of these drops.</p>\n<p>You have the table <b>payouts</b>, which describes the weekly payouts of drivers. It has the following columns:</p>\n<ul><li><code>driver_id</code> - the ID of the driver;</li><li><code>week_start</code> - the Monday of the week (date);</li><li><code>amount</code> - the payout of that week (numeric). A driver has at most one row per week, and weeks where a driver did not work have no row.</li></ul>\n<p>For each row, the <b>previous active week</b> is the latest earlier week of the same driver that has a row, even if it is several weeks before. The <b>drop</b> is <code>(previous amount - amount) / previous amount</code>, in percent.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return the rows where the drop is strictly greater than <code>30</code> percent, with <code>driver_id</code>, <code>week_start</code>, <code>previous_amount</code>, <code>amount</code> and <code>drop_pct</code> rounded to 1 decimal. Rows with no previous active week, or with a previous amount of <code>0</code>, are never returned. Sort by <code>driver_id</code>, then <code>week_start</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p>Table <code>payouts</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>driver_id</th><th>week_start</th><th>amount</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>2026-03-02</td><td>1000</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>2026-03-09</td><td>600</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2026-03-02</td><td>800</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2026-03-16</td><td>700</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2026-03-23</td><td>300</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2026-03-09</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2026-03-16</td><td>100</td></tr></table></div><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>driver_id</th><th>week_start</th><th>previous_amount</th><th>amount</th><th>drop_pct</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>2026-03-09</td><td>1000.0</td><td>600.0</td><td>40.0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2026-03-23</td><td>700.0</td><td>300.0</td><td>57.1</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 10 seconds (psql)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 1 GB<br><b>[input]</b> tables <code>payouts</code><br><b>[output]</b> the result of one query with exactly the columns <code>driver_id</code>, <code>week_start</code>, <code>previous_amount</code>, <code>amount</code>, <code>drop_pct</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"/* Write your PostgreSQL query here */\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["LAG","PARTITION BY","division entière","ROUND numeric"],"minutes":15,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>query.sql</code>. Efface le commentaire de départ si tu veux.</li><li>Écris <b>une seule requête</b> qui commence par <code>SELECT</code> (ou <code>WITH</code>). Les tables <code>payouts</code> existent déjà, remplies avec les données du test : pas de <code>CREATE TABLE</code> ni d'<code>INSERT</code>.</li><li>Le moteur est PostgreSQL. Si tu écris plusieurs requêtes, seul le résultat de la dernière est noté.</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Le résultat de ta requête doit avoir <b>exactement 5 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>driver_id</code>, <code>week_start</code>, <code>previous_amount</code>, <code>amount</code>, <code>drop_pct</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Nomme chaque colonne calculée avec <code>AS</code>.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>ORDER BY</code> à la fin de la requête).</li><li>Valeurs : les nombres sont comparés à 0,000001 près : arrondis seulement là où l'énoncé le demande, et au nombre de décimales demandé ; les dates s'affichent AAAA-MM-JJ.</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 7 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"tables":{"payouts":{"columns":[["driver_id","INT"],["week_start","DATE"],["amount","NUMERIC(10,2)"]],"rows":[[1,"2026-03-02",1000],[1,"2026-03-09",600],[2,"2026-03-02",800],[2,"2026-03-16",700],[2,"2026-03-23",300],[3,"2026-03-09",0],[3,"2026-03-16",100]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["driver_id","week_start","previous_amount","amount","drop_pct"],"rows":[[1,"2026-03-09",1000.0,600.0,40.0],[2,"2026-03-23",700.0,300.0,57.1]]}},{"input":{"tables":{"payouts":{"columns":[["driver_id","INT"],["week_start","DATE"],["amount","NUMERIC(10,2)"]],"rows":[[5,"2026-01-05",100],[5,"2026-01-12",70],[5,"2026-02-02",40],[6,"2026-01-05",50]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["driver_id","week_start","previous_amount","amount","drop_pct"],"rows":[[5,"2026-02-02",70.0,40.0,42.9]]}}],"hidden_n":7,"hidden":"","solution":""}